WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
MCP capabilities commonly appear as tools, resources, and prompts.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.400
A tool is a callable operation exposed by a server.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.525
It may look up information, query a system, create a ticket, summarize a file,

00:00:11.525 --> 00:00:14.100
trigger a workflow, or perform another defined action.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.960
A resource is data or content made available as context, such as a file, schema, record,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.300
document, repository item, or application-specific object.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:26.565
A prompt is a reusable prompt template or interaction pattern exposed by a server.

00:00:26.565 --> 00:00:29.870
Tools and resources are easy to confuse, but they serve different roles.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:33.170
A resource gives the AI application content to reason over.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.395
A tool does something or requests something.

00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:39.320
Reading an API schema as context is different from calling an operation that changes a

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:39.900
system.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.400
Fetching a document is different from updating a ticket.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.035
Good courseware, documentation,

00:00:44.035 --> 00:00:47.700
and system design should make those distinctions visible because the risks and approval

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:48.595
needs are different.

00:00:48.595 --> 00:00:52.000
Prompts are also distinct from user messages and system instructions.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.900
A prompt template may help standardize how a task is framed,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.195
but it should not be treated as a replacement for policy or access control.

00:00:58.195 --> 00:01:02.595
Tool descriptions, resource metadata, and prompt templates all influence model behavior.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:06.100
They affect what the model thinks a capability is for, when it should be used,

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.900
and how the result should be interpreted.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.550
That makes capability metadata operationally important.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.090
Capabilities should be clear, narrow, documented, and understandable.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.080
A narrowly described read-only lookup is easier to review than a broad tool that can

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.150
perform many unrelated actions.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:23.980
Documentation should explain purpose, owner, inputs, outputs, permissions,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.980
data sensitivity, and expected use.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.400
This is not just tidiness.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.250
Clear capability boundaries reduce confusion, make approval decisions easier,

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:35.100
and help teams scale MCP adoption without losing track of what each integration can

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:35.900
actually do.
